    <marc:subfield code="u">003359</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="700" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">Brunner, J W</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="u">000369</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="700" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">Margiotta, A</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="u">010072</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="710" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="g">ANTARES</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="856" ind1="4" ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="u">http://antares.in2p3.fr/index.html</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="y">Experiment home page</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="903" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="s">Data Taking</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="905" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="p">Carr, J</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="905" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="p">Coyle, P A</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="905" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="p">Kouchner, A D</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="916" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="s">n</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="w">200046</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="960" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">50</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="961" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="c">20080909</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="h">0511</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="l">IEX01</marc:subfield>
    <marc:subfield code="x">20001107</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="963" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">PUBLIC</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="970" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">001000677IEX</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="980" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">GREYBOOK</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
  <marc:datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <marc:subfield code="a">The ANTARES (Astronomy with a Neutrino Telescope and ${Abyss}$ environmental RESearch) deep-sea neutrino telescope is designed to search for neutrinos of astrophysical origin. Neutrinos are unique probes of the high energy universe; being neutral they are not deflected by magnetic fields and interacting weakly they can readily escape from the densest regions of the universe. Potential sources of neutrino are galactic (e.g supernova remnants, micro-quasars) and extra-galactic (e.g active galactic nuclei, gamma-ray bursters). Annihilation of dark matter particles in the Sun or Galactic Centre is another well motivated potential source of extra terrestrial neutrinos. The ANTARES detector is located 40 km off the coast of Toulon (France) at a depth of 2475m in the Mediterranean Sea. Being located in the Northern hemisphere it studies the Southern sky and in particular has the Galactic Centre in its field of view. Since 2006, the detector has operated continuously in a partial configuration. The detector was completed in May 2008. It is the most neutrino detector currently operating in the Northern hemisphere. The detection principle exploits the measurement of Cherenkov light induced by relativistic muons resulting from charged-current neutrino interactions in the matter surrounding the telescope. Accurate measurements of the photon arrival times, deposited charge and positions of the photo-sensors are required to determine the direction of neutrino tracks with good angular resolution. Distinguishing upward-going tracks from downward-going tracks helps to reduce the large background due to muons from atmospheric cosmic ray showers to a manageable level. The Cherenkov photons are detected in a three dimensional array of about 900 photomultiplier tubes (PMT) distributed along 12 vertical detection lines. The lines, each 450 m high, are spread over an area of 200 x 200 m$^{2}$ with distance of $\approx$ 60 m between lines. Each line comprises 25 triplets of 10"PMTs. The PMT's are oriented downwards at 45Â° to enhance the sensitivity for upgoing muno tracks. The apparatus measures the arrival time of the Cherenkov photons with nano-second precision. The positions of the photo-sensors are measured with an acoustics positioning system, tiltmeters and compasses to a precision better than 10 cm. Each PMT typically measures a singles rate of 60-100 kHz, mostly single photo-electrons produced by $^{40}$K decay, and occasional bursts of bioluninescence generated by deep-sea fauna. Munon tracks are reconstructed from time correlated hits by a linear fit through the space and time coordinates of photon hits and a subsequent Maximum Likelihood fit based on a probability density function from Monte Carlo simulations. Above energies of 10 TeV the expected angular resolution is &lt;0.3Â°. The ANTARES detector incorporates AMADEUS (Antares Modules for ${Acoustic}$ ${DEtection}$ Under the Sea), a test-bed system to evaluate the potential for acoustic detection of ultra-high energy neutrinos employing acoustic sensors detecting the pressure wave produced in neutrino-included cascades. The ANTARES infrastructure also serves as a deep-sea marine observatory for interdisciplinary sciences such as oceanography,seismology, marine biology and environmental monitoring.</marc:subfield>
  </marc:datafield>
</marc:record>
